sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch04-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Fixation preserves the general outward form of small organisms and cells, but the minute internal networks and granules described as the structure of protoplasm are to a large extent artefacts of coagulating fixatives, although larger structures such as nucleus, vacuoles, centrosomes and the spindle are really present in the living cell.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Fine internal networks and granules seen in fixed cells are largely artefacts";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IV, “On the Internal Form and Structure of the Cell”; topic “Fixation artefacts and living structure”. Scope: histological preparations of cells fixed with chemical reagents"
}
